1
00:00:00,594 --> 00:00:04,663
This is IssueScout, my entry for the Mozilla Otari hackathon.

2
00:00:04,663 --> 00:00:09,462
It's an open-source agent that triages GitHub issues using only open-weight models,

3
00:00:09,462 --> 00:00:11,856
and every single model call goes through Otari.

4
00:00:13,691 --> 00:00:17,214
Maintainers spend hours every week on first-pass triage.

5
00:00:17,214 --> 00:00:20,168
Is it a bug? A duplicate? Does it reproduce?

6
00:00:20,168 --> 00:00:24,486
Is there already a fix? IssueScout does that first pass, and never writes to your

7
00:00:24,486 --> 00:00:25,793
repo unless you tell it to.

8
00:00:27,647 --> 00:00:31,442
Here's the pipeline: six roles, each with its own model chain,

9
00:00:31,442 --> 00:00:36,533
behind one Otari key. Llama Guard 4 screens the untrusted issue text.

10
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:38,988
Qwen3 Embedding finds duplicates.

11
00:00:38,988 --> 00:00:44,306
Qwen3 30B A3B classifies the issue, with labels and a priority,

12
00:00:44,306 --> 00:00:50,545
in JSON mode. DeepSeek V4.1 Flash reproduces bugs in Otari's code sandbox,

13
00:00:50,545 --> 00:00:55,658
and researches with Otari web search, web fetch, and a DeepWiki MCP server.

14
00:00:55,658 --> 00:00:58,783
A writer drafts the reply, and the guard checks the draft again.

15
00:01:00,624 --> 00:01:04,613
Let's run it for real. issuescout doctor asks Otari which built-in tools this

16
00:01:04,613 --> 00:01:09,590
workspace has: web fetch, web search and code execution are all ok.

17
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:11,772
Then it prints each role's model chain.

18
00:01:11,772 --> 00:01:15,442
The classifier and writer chains start with Otari routing policies,

19
00:01:15,442 --> 00:01:16,715
not raw model names.

20
00:01:18,569 --> 00:01:23,717
The classifier policy routes Qwen3 30B, falling back to Ministral 8B.

21
00:01:23,717 --> 00:01:30,080
The writer policy routes DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, falling back to Qwen3 235B.

22
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:34,376
Otari does that failover server-side and reports it in the Otari-Fallback header.

23
00:01:36,191 --> 00:01:40,373
If a policy is missing or a model is refused, the client walks the rest of the

24
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:44,157
chain. From every response it records the serving model, provider,

25
00:01:44,157 --> 00:01:48,168
fallback flag, attempt count, cost, and Otari tool calls.

26
00:01:48,168 --> 00:01:49,816
That's the trace you'll see in the report.

27
00:01:51,658 --> 00:01:55,453
Now a live run on the two newest issues in Otari's own repo.

28
00:01:55,453 --> 00:01:58,464
It's a dry run, so GitHub is only read.

29
00:01:58,464 --> 00:02:03,089
The user flag tags every request for per-user attribution, and max spend is a

30
00:02:03,089 --> 00:02:06,646
client-side cap. The real run took about fifty seconds:

31
00:02:06,646 --> 00:02:08,930
eleven Otari calls, about two cents.

32
00:02:10,769 --> 00:02:15,678
Budgets work on two levels. In Otari, a monthly workspace spend ceiling and a daily

33
00:02:15,678 --> 00:02:20,155
per-member budget. In the client, a ledger adds up the cost on every response and

34
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:26,485
refuses calls past the cap. If Otari answers 402, IssueScout stops cleanly and keeps

35
00:02:26,485 --> 00:02:31,451
rule-based results. And every request carries a safety identifier plus user,

36
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:32,860
role and run metadata.

37
00:02:34,714 --> 00:02:36,214
Here's the report from that run.

38
00:02:36,214 --> 00:02:42,259
Issue 2742, a sign-in failure in private windows, is a P1 bug with three labels,

39
00:02:42,259 --> 00:02:46,395
and the guard says safe. The related links came from Otari web search.

40
00:02:46,395 --> 00:02:51,395
Then the drafted reply: polite, specific, and asking for exactly what's needed next.

41
00:02:53,236 --> 00:02:58,383
And the Otari gateway trace. The classifier policy was served by Qwen3 30B on

42
00:02:58,383 --> 00:03:01,963
Nebius; the writer policy by DeepSeek on DeepInfra.

43
00:03:01,963 --> 00:03:04,167
The web search query is listed too.

44
00:03:04,167 --> 00:03:08,588
That one-cent search is most of the bill; the other calls cost fractions of a cent.

45
00:03:08,588 --> 00:03:14,895
Issue 2741, the quickstart URL, came back as a P2 bug, labeled api,

46
00:03:14,895 --> 00:03:15,804
docs and quickstart.

47
00:03:17,636 --> 00:03:22,204
Research is one request: otari web search, capped with max uses,

48
00:03:22,204 --> 00:03:27,795
otari web fetch, and the DeepWiki MCP server, by workspace id or inline with a tool

49
00:03:27,795 --> 00:03:31,795
allow-list. Otari runs the whole tool loop and returns its sources.

50
00:03:33,636 --> 00:03:38,317
When a bug report includes a Python snippet, the reproducer runs it in Otari's code

51
00:03:38,317 --> 00:03:44,511
execution sandbox. On python colorlog issue 143, DeepSeek installed colorlog,

52
00:03:44,511 --> 00:03:49,056
ran the snippet, and confirmed that ANSI colors leak into the no-color formatter.

53
00:03:49,056 --> 00:03:50,408
The bug really reproduces.

54
00:03:52,224 --> 00:03:56,917
Chat Completions doesn't list sandbox calls, so IssueScout checks the billed cost.

55
00:03:56,917 --> 00:04:00,281
No sandbox fee means the repro is marked unverified.

56
00:04:02,158 --> 00:04:05,613
Fallbacks, live. I put a fake model at the front of the writer chain.

57
00:04:05,613 --> 00:04:10,101
Otari rejects it with a 400, the client falls back to the writer policy,

58
00:04:10,101 --> 00:04:13,556
DeepSeek serves the reply, and the trace records both.

59
00:04:13,556 --> 00:04:15,488
That run cost three hundredths of a cent.

60
00:04:19,658 --> 00:04:22,839
Sixteen tests run with Otari and GitHub mocked.

61
00:04:22,839 --> 00:04:25,067
One feeds in a prompt-injection issue:

62
00:04:25,067 --> 00:04:29,521
the guard flags it, it gets needs-human-review, and no other model sees that text.

63
00:04:31,369 --> 00:04:36,187
To recap: open-weight models in distinct roles, routing policies and fallbacks,

64
00:04:36,187 --> 00:04:40,744
web search and fetch, DeepWiki MCP, sandboxed code execution,

65
00:04:40,744 --> 00:04:44,926
budgets and spend caps, per-user tagging, and an open-model guardrail,

66
00:04:44,926 --> 00:04:45,948
each mapped to the code.

67
00:04:47,794 --> 00:04:51,403
IssueScout is MIT licensed and dry-run by default.

68
00:04:51,403 --> 00:04:54,976
The code is on GitHub, on the otari-hackathon branch.

69
00:04:54,976 --> 00:04:55,601
Thanks for watching.
